      "In 1847 readers of Jane Eyre encountered a powerful narrative voice identified on the title page not as Charlotte Brontë but as Currer Bell. The novel had been sent into the world; the person behind it had not been fully introduced. This was not a failure of expression. It was a deliberate separation between the work and the identity by which it might be judged.",
      "Charlotte lived in the Yorkshire parsonage at Haworth with her sisters Emily and Anne, who were also writers. Their access to books and their habits of imaginative composition developed in a household where intellectual activity mattered. Yet Victorian publishing belonged to a public world in which a woman's name could affect assumptions about seriousness, respectability and literary style.",
