      "In 1534 Thomas More, formerly Lord Chancellor of England, was asked to swear an oath connected with Henry VIII's new settlement of succession and religious authority. He refused. More did not simply launch into a public declaration of opposition. At points he guarded his precise reasons, leaving silence to do work that a spoken explanation might make more dangerous.",
      "The crisis had grown from the king's determination to end his marriage to Catherine of Aragon and establish a new succession. Parliamentary measures and the growing claim of royal supremacy changed the relationship between the English crown and the Church of Rome. To refuse an official oath could now be understood as resistance to the political order.",
